question:
How does your organization define thresholds at which severe risks, unless adequately mitigated, may be considered "unreasonable"? Please describe the framework(s) most relevant to your organization's role and context. If your organisation does not use formal thresholds, you may describe instead the policies, decision criteria, or governance processes you apply when assessing severe AI risks. You may also describe:
• Specific examples of contexts in which your models or systems could, in your assessment, pose unreasonable or intolerable risk;
• How mitigations are graduated by capability or propensity level;
• How your organisation considers the specificities of different AI technologies, such as generative AI and agentic AI, including any specific risk assessment or governance mechanisms applied to agentic AI capabilities.

How does your organization define thresholds at which severe risks, unless adequately mitigated, may be considered "unreasonable"? Please describe the framework(s) most relevant to your organization's role and context. If your organisation does not use formal thresholds, you may describe instead the policies, decision criteria, or governance processes you apply when assessing severe AI risks. You may also describe:
• Specific examples of contexts in which your models or systems could, in your assessment, pose unreasonable or intolerable risk;
• How mitigations are graduated by capability or propensity level;
• How your organisation considers the specificities of different AI technologies, such as generative AI and agentic AI, including any specific risk assessment or governance mechanisms applied to agentic AI capabilities.

answer:
Decision authority. Deployment decisions rest with the CTO, supported by the two technical leads (System Architect and Lead Software/AI Engineer). For client-facing systems, deployment additionally requires explicit client acceptance following user acceptance testing (UAT) — no AI feature moves to production on MGOIT's decision alone.
Evidence and criteria required before deployment:
• Functional and performance validation in a staging environment mirroring production, using anonymised or synthetic data;
• Edge-case and adversarial testing of AI outputs, including checks for bias, incorrect or fabricated outputs, and failure modes on malformed inputs;
• Security review consistent with our ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls: access control, encryption in transit and at rest, secrets management, and data-flow review (what data reaches which model provider, under what terms);
• Privacy and compliance check: GDPR lawful basis, data minimisation, and anonymisation applied to any personal data in AI pipelines;
• Confirmation that graduated human-review mechanisms (per our risk criteria) are implemented and functioning, not merely designed.
Interpreting evaluation results. We treat evaluation metrics as context-bound: results obtained on test data are considered indicative, not guarantees, particularly for generative components where output quality varies with real-world input distribution. Known limitations (e.g. domains where the model underperforms, language coverage, edge cases routed to human review) are documented and communicated to the client as operating caveats, not hidden.
Sign-offs. Internal: technical lead responsible for the system signs off on testing evidence; CTO approves the release. External: client sign-off after UAT for client-operated systems.
Safeguards required at deployment:
• Phased release where feasible — limited user group or parallel run alongside the existing process before full cutover;
• Production monitoring and logging of AI outputs and agent actions, with alerting on anomalous behaviour;
• Defined rollback path allowing the AI feature to be disabled without taking down the surrounding system;
• Support and incident-response commitments (orientative SLAs of ~4h for critical and ~24h for major issues during the support period), under which AI-related incidents are triaged like any other production incident, with the added option of reverting to human-only workflow.
